Rob here with a GS news update former  Panamanian dictator Manuel Noriega has  brought Activision a lawsuit over 2012's  call of duty black ops 2 Noriega is  taking legal action against the  publisher for the blatant misuse of  unlawful exploitation and  misappropriation for economic gain of  his image in the popular game Noriega  was the military dictator of Panama from  1983 until 1989 when he was ousted the  80 year old who is now serving a prison  sentence in Panama claims in his lawsuit  in an effort to increase the popularity  and revenue generated by black ops to  activision used without authorization or  consent the image and likeness of Manuel  Noriega in black ops 2 the former  dictator goes on to say that call duty  black ops 2 depicts him as a kidnapper  murderer and enemy of the state Noriega  was convicted of crimes in the USA  including drug trafficking racketeering  and money laundering then he was  extradited to France and charged for  murder before being extradited back to  Panama where he was charged for murder  and human rights abuses he is currently  still in prison Noriega is seeking  damages for the unjust enrichment unfair  business practices and violation of  common law publicity rights in addition  Noriega hopes to collect punitive  damages for lost profits and other  charges according to the lawsuit  gamespot user Rican V has stated in the  comments he has a better case than Lohan  what do you think stay tuned to gamespot  for all of your gaming news  you
 

